\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v all my choices are in both Web 11 and AHED 4\line}My standard reference for spelling and word usage is \b \i The American Heritage Dictionary\b0 \i0 , Fifth Edition (unabridged), 2011 (\b \i American Heritage\b0 \i0  is listed in \b \i CMS\b0 \i0 \~16 section\~7.1 as an acceptable choice).}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I've decided I much prefer \b \i AHD\b0 \i0  to \b \i WEB\~11\b0 ,\i0  which had been my choice for some earlier novels.  Note that \b \i AHD\b0 \i0  relied on a usage panel of professional writers, including, when he was alive, Isaac Asimov; the panel was chaired by linguist Steven Pinker.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 You can access this dictionary online for free:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 \b (((https://ahdictionary.com))))&|&& URL-ahd &&||&& TXT-ahd &&|||&\b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v Wednesday, August 22, 2012, in Mississauga:\line}{\v \u9484? In general, I find it wrong that authors have to adhere to a\line}{\v \u9474? specific dictionary -- but copyeditors demand that.  American\line}{\v \u9474? Heritage only allows "nightclub," not "night club."  Random House\line}{\v \u9474? Webster's allows both.  And for the drink, AH allows only\line}{\v \u9492? "Scotch," whereas RHW allows both "Scotch and "scotch."\line}{\v \u9484? But if you've said American Heritage is your authority, then you\line}{\v \u9474? have to do it American Heritage's way -- which changes the\line}{\v \u9474? balance of power.  Dictionary compilers are supposed to watch the\line}{\v \u9474? way careful professional writers use the language and faithfully\line}{\v \u9474? record that usage, but this forces authors to endorse a\line}{\v \u9474? dictionary, making the lexicographers, not the writers, the ones\line}{\v \u9492? choosing how language evolves.\line}{\v \u9484? CMS16: 7.2 Spellings peculiar to particular disciplines: Where a\line}{\v \u9474? variant spelling carries a special connotation within a\line}{\v \u9474? discipline, the author's preference should be respected. For\line}{\v \u9474? example, "archeology," though it is listed as an equal variant of\line}{\v \u9474? "archaeology" in Webster's, is the spelling insisted on by\line}{\v \u9474? certain specialists. In the absence of such a preference, Chicago\line}{\v \u9474? prefers the first-listed "archaeology." (Webster's separates\line}{\v \u9492? equal variants by or; secondary variants are preceded by also.)\line}{\v modified on Tuesday, October 13, 2015, after copyedit on QN was done\line}{\v "paleontology" to "palaeontology,"\line}However, although \b \i The Chicago Manual of Style\b0 \i0  may advise to "normally opt for" the first spelling listed in the dictionary of choice, I consider the selection between variant spellings (such as "aesthetics" and "esthetics" or "advisor" and "adviser") to be the author's prerogative.  I prefer the former spellings in each of the foregoing pairs, and also prefer "caf\u233?" to "cafe," "clich\u233?" to "cliche," "cross-section" to "cross section," "na\u239?ve" to "naive," "na\u239?vet\u233?" to "naivet\u233?," and "disruptor" (well established as the usual spelling in science-fictional contexts) to "disrupter."  If my preferred spelling is listed in my preferred dictionary, whether as an equal variant (which \b \i American Heritage\b0 \i0  shows following the word "or") or an unequal variant (which \b \i American Heritage\b0 \i0  shows following the word "also"), please don't change it to another form.  Note that I use both the terms "atomic bomb" and "atom bomb" deliberately; please do not regularize.}\par
