\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v \u9484? I consider the earlier but larger Webster's Third New\line}{\v \u9474? International Dictionary to be both far too permissive and\line}{\v \u9474? significantly dated.  If a word isn't in Web11, I defer not to\line}{\v \u9474? Webster's Third but rather to Webster's New World Dictionary,\line}{\v \u9492? Fourth Edition.\line}}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 \b \i PERIOD FLAVOR\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep{\f0\fs24 \cf1 This novel is set mostly in the 1940s and 1950s.  Spelling conventions have changed in the last seventy-five-odd years, largely at the bullying behest of the \b \i Chicago Manual of Style.\b0 \i0   To retain the period flavor of this novel, I've deliberately chosen to hyphenate many prefixes and suffixes that in contemporary works might be set solid.  For instance, J. Robert Oppenheimer's actual job title, in all historical documents, was "Co-ordinator of Rapid Rupture" (not "Coordinator").  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
