\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 For the same reason, I'm also retaining the diphthong in \b archaeology\b0  and \b palaeontology\b0 , and am writing \b policy maker\b0 , \b space flight\b0 , and \b space ship\b0  as two words.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 Today, we would commonly write of a voyage "from the Earth to the Moon and then on to Mars."  However, prior to the Space Age, our planet and its natural satellite were almost always referred to by uncapitalized nouns, and I've adhered to that usage in the text:  "from the earth to the moon and then on to Mars."}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 Those long-gone decades predate modern attempts (of which in other contexts I heartily approve) toward gender-neutrality and gender-inclusivity in language.  Therefore, I'm adhering here to the old practices of using \b blond\b0  for males and \b blonde\b0  for females (as both adjective and noun), and using only "he" where today we might write "he or she."}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I'm also treating "\b Jr.\b0 " as a parenthetical, as it was back then:  Leslie Groves, Jr., not Leslie Groves Jr. (which is what the 17th edition of \b \i CMS\b0 \i0  would have us write).}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I've chosen to include the periods in acronyms and academic degrees, as they would indeed have been commonly styled this way in the 1940s and 1950s:}\par
