\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v SPELLING EXCEPTIONS\line}{\v \u9484? It's my contention that "lifeform," "airlock," and "spacetime"\line}{\v \u9474? are single words in science-fictional contexts; please leave them\line}{\v \u9474? as such.  Further, I hold that "email," "ebook," "ecommerce," and\line}{\v \u9474? "website" (solid single words, all lowercase) are the correct\line}{\v \u9474? spellings -- or will be in the future in which my book is set;\line}{\v \u9474? please do not alter my usage.  Finally, "light-year" is a\line}{\v \u9474? technical term and, like "foot-pound," always takes a hyphen;\line}{\v \u9474? please do not change this (AHD5 is wrong on this point; WEB11\line}{\v \u9492? is correct).\line}{\v Please leave the Canadian spellings intact in these proper nouns:\line}{\v @@ bulleted list does not convert properly\line}{\v \u8729? Harbourfront (Toronto neighborhood)\line}{\v \u8729? Winnipeg Centre of the Royal Astronomical Society of Canada\line}{\v Please leave these as is:\line}{\v @@ bulleted list does not convert properly\line}\b \i ITALICS\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I use italics for:}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v @@ bulleted list does not convert properly\line}Emphasis}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 The titles of publications, movies, and television series}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 Some verbatim transcriptions of thoughts, especially when there might be ambiguity about whether the text is thought or narration}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 All non-English words and phrases being consciously used as non-English, and all Latin phrases (which are usually affectations) even if they appear in English dictionaries: \i "au\~revoir"\i0  and \i "en\~route"\i0  (French), \i "a\~cappella"\i0  (Italian), and \i "per\~se"\i0  (Latin) are all italicized.  Please note however that I do \i not\i0  italicize foreign-source single words (not phrases) that have been wholly co-opted into standard English, and whose usage doesn't constitute an affectation: words such as "milieu" or "liaison" (French) and "trek" (Afrikaans) are not italicized, but \i bon\~voyage\i0  -- being a phrase -- is.  (My rationale is that "au," "revoir," "en," "cappella," "bon," and so on are not valid English words on their own, and only the italicizing linking them to the preceding or following text turns them into a meaningful phrase.)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 \b \i The Chicago Manual of Style\b0 \i0  14th Edition (1993), section 5.4, says, "Generally, punctuation marks are printed in the same style or font of type as the word, letter, character, or symbol immediately preceding them."}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I agree with this.  However, starting with \b \i CMS\b0 \i0 's 15th Edition (2003), section 6.3 ("Punctuation and font: primary system"), changed this, saying: "All punctuation marks should appear in the same font -- roman or italic -- as the main or surrounding text, except for punctuation that belongs to a title or an exclamation in a different font."  (A similar point is made in \b \i CMS\b0 \i0 's 16th edition in section 6.2.)}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v Smith played the title role in Hamlet, Macbeth, and King Lear; after his final performance, during which many in the audience wept, he announced his retirement.\line}{\v Many editors admire Wired Style: it is both elegant and easy to use.\line}{\v An Apache Life-way: The Economic, Social, and Religious Institutions of the Chiricahua Indians\line}{\v Are you saying the wound was self-inflicted?\line}{\v She is the author of What Next?\line}{\v For light entertainment he reads King Lear!\line}{\v The manual Online! is always at my elbow.\line}{\v We heard his cries of "Help!"\line}}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I prefer the older standard set by \b \i CMS\b0 \i0 's 14th Edition (or as still allowed for print publications under \b \i CMS\b0 \i0 \~16 section 6.4 ("Punctuation and Font -- aesthetic considerations")) as it does indeed respect the aesthetics of how type actually looks on the printed page.  In particular I want question marks, exclamation marks, colons, and semicolons immediately following italicized words to always be italicized, along with any closing quotation mark that follows them: \i Look out!\i0  not \i Look out\i0 ! and "Say \i what?"\i0  not "Say \i what\i0 ?" or "Say \i what?\i0 "}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v added Friday, April 20, 2012, in Winnipeg\line}\ulw \ulnone \ulw \ulnone \ulw EXCEPTIONS:\ulnone \ulw \ulnone \ulw \ulnone }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 Although the names of sailing vessels and spaceships are italicized, if they are being used in the possessive form the following apostrophe and the letter \i s\i0  are \i not\i0  italicized: "the \i Titanic\i0 's crew."}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I do not italicize an em dash even if it immediately follows italicized text, unless the em dash is the end of a paragraph or is included in italicized dialogue.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 If the first word in a sentence is italicized, and that word is preceded by a quotation mark, I also italicize that quotation mark.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 When letters of the alphabet appear as words in the manuscript, I've italicized them except (per \b \i CMS\b0 \i0 \~16 section\~7.63) when the letters are meant to suggest shapes: He drew a \i T\i0  in Scrabble while he was wearing a T-shirt.  However, I don't italicize letters when they are used for spelling: "That word is spelled c-a-t."}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v \u9484? For the boldfaced names of people in the Acknowledgments, I have\line}{\v \u9474? not bolded any immediately following punctuation; this is a\line}{\v \u9492? deliberate aesthetic choice.\line}}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 \b \i PUNCTUATION\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I use serial (Oxford) commas.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I use commas before terminal \i too\i0  and around internal \i too,\i0  but I don't use commas with close appositives when the name is a single word: I write "her husband John" but, except in long lists (as in the Acknowledgments), "her husband, John Smith."}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I deliberately do not precede "suddenly" with a comma; the comma negates the rapid effect.  I write, "Suddenly a bomb exploded," not "Suddenly, a bomb exploded."}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 Except for Biblical and Classical names, I form the possessive of names ending in \i s\i0  by adding apostrophe-s.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I use three (never four) periods for ellipsis points.  My rationale is that an ellipsis in dialog indicates a trailing off (an \i incomplete\i0  sentence, which, since it is incomplete, has no terminal punctuation).  Likewise, sentence fragments ending in an em dash (denoting an interruption or abrupt change of thought) are also incomplete and therefore are not followed by terminal punctuation.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I almost always hyphenate compound adjectives (phrasal adjectives).  }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v \u9484? , except for the exceptions enumerated in CMS15 section 7.90.\line}{\v \u9474? In particular, I follow that section in leaving most phrases\line}{\v \u9474? about color open: "emerald green, bluish green, coal black, a\line}{\v \u9474? green and red dress, a black-and-white print, the truth isn't\line}{\v \u9474? black and white.  (Open whether before or after a noun except for\line}{\v \u9474? such established expressions as black-and-white, which are\line}{\v \u9474? usually hyphenated before a noun.)"  By extension from the CMS\line}{\v \u9474? examples, I also don't hyphenate color terms beginning with\line}{\v \u9474? "dark," "light," "pale," or similar terms: dark blue, light\line}{\v \u9492? pink, pale green.\line}}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 However, I do \i not\i0  treat color specifications as components of compound adjectives.  I write "green phosphor line," not "green-phosphor line," "orange vinyl upholstery," not "orange-vinyl upholstery," and so on.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v CMS 16 online relegates this note to a PDF table with no section number:\line}{\v https://www.chicagomanualofstyle.org/16/images/ch07_tab01.pdf\line}Please note that according to \b \i CMS\b0 \i0 \~15 section 7.90, compound chemical terms are \i not\i0  hyphenated, even when they are being used as adjectives: it is correct to write, as I do, "carbon dioxide gas."}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v \u9484? Please note, too, that this novel proposes an invention called\line}{\v \u9474? the "quantum tuning fork," which I have consciously chosen to\line}{\v \u9492? style without a hyphen.\line}{\v added Tuesday, October 13, 2015, after copyediting\line}{\v https://www.facebook.com/robertjsawyer/posts/10153571108601013?pnref=story\line}I do not usually employ the genitive or possessive case with gerunds in constructions such as in the following examples (although in dialogue individual characters may deviate from this); pedantry may be appropriate in academic contexts, but it can be disruptively formal in fiction.  I write "Despite Atlanta also being home to CNN" not "Despite Atlanta's also being home to CNN," and "Recall Dr. Goldsmith giving testimony" not "Recall Dr. Goldsmith's giving testimony."  Please do not adjust my usage.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v !! Novel 24 (added Saturday, January 14, 2017)\line}I have set as closed pairs or triplets of initials in a person's name:  "I.I. Rabi," not "I.\~I. Rabi," for Isidor Isaac Rabi; "E.O. Lawrence," not "E.\~O. Lawrence," "J.R.R. Tolkien," not "J.\~R.\~R.\~Tolkien," and so on.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v https://web.archive.org/web/20090429032708/http://www.nyu.edu:80/classes/copyXediting/Hyphens.html\line}{\v \u9484? Likewise, I often put a hyphen before a suffix, especially when\line}{\v \u9474? appending the suffix "like."  Novel coinages are much easier to\line}{\v \u9474? read with the hyphen than without: "fin-like" rather than\line}{\v \u9474? "finlike," "cat-like" rather than "catlike," "sausage-like"\line}{\v \u9492? rather than "sausagelike," and so on.\line}{\v HYPHENATION OF PREFIXES AND SUFFIXES\line}{\v preeminent\line}{\v \u9484? I often put a hyphen after a prefix, especially when setting the\line}{\v \u9474? prefix solid would result in the same vowel appearing twice in a\line}{\v \u9474? row (and I never set a prefix ending in "e" solid with a word\line}{\v \u9474? beginning with "e"), and I also retain the hyphen in many novel\line}{\v \u9474? coinages or technical terms; in this, I prefer the standard set\line}{\v \u9474? by the Associated Press to that of CMS.  Please leave the hyphen\line}{\v \u9474? intact in terms such as "re-entry," "re-elect," "post-retinal,"\line}{\v \u9474? "pre-existing," "co-conspirator," and "co-worker," as they are\line}{\v \u9474? much easier to read than "reentry," "reelect," "postretinal,"\line}{\v \u9492? "coconspirator," and "coworker."\line}{\v added Wednesday, August 22, 2012, in Mississauga\line}}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 \b \i SPLIT INFINITIVES\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I'm aware of the supposed prohibition against split infinitives; however, I don't agree with it.  Please do not adjust my usage; I prefer to boldly go where a great many writers have gone before\~...}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 \b \i CAPITALIZATION\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I use lower-case for the first letter in a sentence following a colon.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v \u9484? I do not normally capitalize pronouns referring to God, except in\line}{\v \u9492? quotations from Scripture.\line}}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 \b \i NUMBERS\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I spell out all numbers that begin sentences; except for dates, spell out zero through one hundred (even when followed by the word "percent"), and use numerals for 101 and beyond, including years ("2019"), their two-digit abbreviations ("'19"), and precise times ("9:30 a.m."); when an ordinal date is mentioned in dialog, I spell it out as a word: I write "March 15, 2019," in normal text, but in dialog would write, "Beware March fifteenth -- it's the Ides of March!"}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 However, when using slang terms referring to rotational turns, I always spell out "one-eighty" and "three-sixty," and in dialog when I want to differentiate the pronoun, I write "a hundred and fifty," rather than "150" (which would normally be read as "one hundred and fifty").}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I don't usually spell out numbers containing decimal points, even in dialog (I use "123.45" rather than "one hundred and twenty-three point four five"). }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v \u9484? However, the number "2.9" has a special meaning in this novel,\line}{\v \u9474? and I do spell it out -- "two-point-nine" -- in dialog; please\line}{\v \u9492? leave that as is.\line}{\v \u9484? I always use numbers, even in dialog, for the "4:2:1" ratio that\line}{\v \u9474? is mentioned repeatedly in this novel; please do not change this\line}{\v \u9492? to the awkward "four-to-two-to-one."\line}}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I do not use apostrophes to pluralize numbers: "1990's" is possessive of the year 1990 ("1990's best novel was ..."), not a reference to the decade that ran from 1990 to 1999 (I write: "The 1990s were a wonderful time").}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 \b \i PROFANITY\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 The term "damn it" is stronger than "dammit."  I use both deliberately for specific effect; please do not adjust my usage.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 \b \i VERBALIZED PAUSES\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I use "um" and "umm" to represent verbalized pauses of different lengths.  I use both deliberately for specific effect; please do not adjust my usage.  I treat "hmmm" as speech, and don't italicize it, unless I'm doing so for emphasis (in other words, sometimes it is italicized and sometimes it isn't, and that's a deliberate choice on my part).}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 \b \i "NOW," "HERE," AND "AND THEN"\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep{\f0\fs24 \cf1 Even when writing in the past tense, I sometimes use the terms "now" and "here."  This is unexceptionable grammatically: \b \i The American Heritage Dictionary\b0 ,\i0  for instance, gives the following as one of the definitions of "now," employing a past-tense example: "At this point in the series of events; then: \i The ship was now listing to port."\i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I use "and then" (including sometimes to begin a sentence) when perhaps either just "and" or "then" could have been employed; I do this for reasons of cadence and personal style.  Please do not modify my usage.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 I have a fondness for stream-of-consciousness narrative devices, including ending one paragraph with "and" followed by an em dash, and beginning the next with an em dash and "and" (with a lowercase \i a\i0 ) followed by a description of a suddenly changed circumstance or mental state.  These devices are employed deliberately; please do not modify them.}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v METRIC VS. IMPERIAL MEASUREMENTS\line}{\v \u9484? My characters use metric or imperial (feet, pounds) measures\line}{\v \u9474? depending on the context and the individual character.  Please do\line}{\v \u9474? not convert my usage.  My Canadian characters may use both\line}{\v \u9474? systems interchangeably; this accurately reflects real Canadian\line}{\v \u9492? usage.\line}\page }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\qc\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 {\v updated through Friday, May 15, 2020\line}\f2\fs36 \b Praise for \i The Oppenheimer Alternative\i0 \f0\fs24 \cf1 \b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "My favorite Sawyer book of all; the extrapolation is as brilliant as the history. An extremely clever science-fictional tale that grows naturally out of the research done by extremely smart people on the way to the development of the atomic age. They will live for you after you are done reading this book; this is what they were really like. A solid award contender!" --\b \i Amazing Stories\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Sawyer does a magnificent job. Every individual pops right off the page, fully integrated both into their era and amongst themselves. The whole book is rich in emotional depth. This fine novel makes explicit its relevance to the present." --\b \i Locus\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "I loved it! I know the history of this period well and I'm one or two degrees of separation from many of these people. Sawyer's portrayals ring true to me. Getting to read this early was a treat!" --\b Lee Smolin\b0 , bestselling author of \b \i The Trouble with Physics\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Impressive use of scientific and historical detail and Sawyer's characterization of the man himself pull back the layers of Oppenheimer's morals, genius, and grief. Science fiction fans will devour this smart speculative tale." --\b \i Publishers Weekly\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "You can always count on Sawyer, a storyteller of the first order, for a good read. \b \i The Oppenheimer Alternative\b0 \i0  delivers on that count, but it's also a book that explores, on a human level, the sort of bigger questions raised by world-changing events, both real and imagined." --\b \i The Toronto Star\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "With typical stylistic verve and a remarkable degree of research, Sawyer has crafted a world that is an apt and accurate reflection of our own while also folding in the shifts and changes that create this alternate reality. \b \i The Oppenheimer Alternative\b0 \i0  is precisely the kind of well-constructed and thoughtful speculative fiction that we've come to expect from Robert J. Sawyer. It's a fascinating premise brought to fruition by one of our most gifted sci-fi authors." --\b \i The Maine Edge\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "An imaginative restructuring of a phantasmagoric life into an alternative phantasmagorical story. Oppenheimer fans will be intrigued." --\b Martin J. Sherwin\b0 , co-author of the Pulitzer Prize-winning biography \b \i American Prometheus: The Triumph and Tragedy of J. Robert Oppenheimer\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "A truly science fictional work of alternate history which turns on the decisions -- and discoveries -- of the great physicists who wrote the history of the 20th century: Einstein, Fermi, G\u246?del, and Oppenheimer. They are the vividly realized, all-too-human characters who people this novel, and give its brilliant speculations human life and blood. I read this book at a gulp, and you should too -- now I'm going to go read it again. Bravo!" --\b \i S.M. Stirling\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "You might think you know what to expect from a Robert J. Sawyer book: something set in a near-future world similar to our own; real people dealing with the social implications of ideas from the edges of current science; fascinating, well-dramatized exploration of philosophical and ethical matters. \b \i The Oppenheimer Alternative\b0 \i0 , an alternate history thriller set in the opening years of the atomic age, might seem a bit of a departure. Not to worry, this book has everything a reader wants from Sawyer. Well-drawn alternate history, rigorous SF thriller, social commentary, redemption narrative -- \b \i The Oppenheimer Alternative\b0 \i0  reimagines one of the most influential lives of the twentieth century. Well worth reading." --\b \i Analog Science Fiction & Fact\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Incredibly realistic: the characters, locations, the era, and even the science. I felt like I was back in Los Alamos -- and I should know: I worked there! Breathlessly riveting; Sawyer pulls it off masterfully." --\b Doug Beason\b0 , former Associate Laboratory Director, Los Alamos National Laboratory}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "The feel and detail of the Manhattan Project figures is deep and well done. I knew many of these physicists, and Sawyer nails them accurately." --\b Gregory Benford\b0 , author of \b \i The Berlin Project\b0 \i0  and Edward Teller's graduate student}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "An excellent blend of hard science, alternate history and the inner workings of one of the key men of the 20th century. Highly recommended." --\b \i Sci-Fi Bulletin\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Hugo and Nebula award-winning author Robert J Sawyer balances effortless prose with extensive research to create a novel so well-crafted that the lines between reality and alternate reality blur. Combining a whole lot of science with a whole lot of speculation, \b \i The Oppenheimer Alternative\b0 \i0  succeeds as an amazing border-hopper between the reality of science and the boundless imagination. It's the perfect addition to hard science fiction collections." --\b \i Aurealis\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "I enjoyed it tremendously! Really great, a page turner. I was hooked from the beginning to the end. Another fine addition to the Sawyer canon!" --\b Andre Bormanis\b0 , producer, \b \i The Orville\b0 \i0  and \b \i Cosmos\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "An expert and engrossing knitting of the threads of history, with genuine characters of the mid 20th Century, to weave out a compulsive fictional narrative." --\b Jem Rolls\b0 , author of the play \b \i The Inventor of All Things\b0 \i0  about Leo Szilard}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Sawyer portrays brilliantly and poignantly the struggles of the scientists who started it all and were consequently obliged to bear an unbearable burden." --\b James Christie\b0 , Chair, \b Project Ploughshares\b0 , member organization of the Nobel Peace Prize-winning International Campaign to Abolish Nuclear Weapons}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Every single character in this book is a real person. That's right, all of them. Even some of the dialogue is real. This is one of the most ambitious books I've come across. I can't imagine the amount of research that went into giving each character a believable voice and personality (actually, I can, the bibliography runs 30 pages on my phone), but this is Robert J. Sawyer so maybe I shouldn't be surprised. In some ways, it reminded me of the play \i Copenhagen\i0  by Michael Frayn, with more thrills, cool rockets and a doomsday scenario to boot." --\b Tor.com\b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "You can call this alternate history or alternate astrophysics (or both). Whatever term you choose, it's a terrific story." --\b Eric Flint\b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "A novel as expansive as the physics (and physicists) under whose watch the original Project \i Orion\i0  was launched." --\b George Dyson\b0 , author of \b \i Project Orion: The Atomic Spaceships 1957-1965\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "A wealth of detail lends Sawyer's novel the grounded believability necessary to pull off a fictionalization of events, speculating on the inner lives of the players, the meaning behind the discoveries they made, and the philosophical impact thereof. Sawyer employs a no-nonsense style reminiscent of that of Isaac Asimov or Ben Bova, which makes the narrative easy to navigate. The result is a realistic and intriguing look at Oppenheimer and his work." --\b \i Booklist\b0 \i0 , journal of the American Library Association}\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "There are some writers who, even if they do not write the same book over and over again, write very similar books. Not so with Robert Sawyer, who pairs an enormous imagination with extensive research. Whether writing about a dinosaur Galileo, atheist Neanderthals, or a self-aware World Wide Web, Sawyer makes his characters, and the world in which they live, come alive. If there is one thing Sawyer's books do have in common, in addition to their overall excellence, it is his concern for morality and determining the right thing to do. In this book, this concern is reflected in discussions over the nuclear bomb and later keeping the end of the world a big secret from ordinary people. Fans of historical novels set in the recent past will love \b \i The Oppenheimer Alternative\b0 \i0 . Fans of alternate history who are willing to wait a while for the speculative content will find their patience rewarded. Readers interested in science, and how scientists interact also will find much to enjoy." --\b \i SFRevu\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Sawyer has outdone himself! No one could have taken on this project with such gusto and with such a search for the truth as this outstanding author. I've never read such a complete and thrilling account of Oppenheimer's world." --\b Jonas Saul\b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 \f2\fs36 \b Praise for Robert J. Sawyer\f0\fs24 \cf1 \b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep\keepn{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "A new Robert J. Sawyer book is always cause for celebration." --\b \i Analog Science Fiction and Fact\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752\keep{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Sawyer not only has an irresistibly engaging narrative voice but also a gift for confronting thorny philosophical conundrums.  At every opportunity, he forces his readers to think while holding their attention with ingenious premises and superlative craftsmanship." --\b \i Booklist\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Can Sawyer write? Yes -- with near-Asimovian clarity, with energy and drive, with such grace that his writing becomes invisible as the story comes to life in your mind." --\b Orson Scott Card\b0 , author of \b \i Ender's Game\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Robert J. Sawyer is by any measure one of the world's leading (and most interesting) science-fiction writers.  His fiction is a fascinating blend of intellectually compelling big ideas and humane, enduring characters." --\b \i The Globe and Mail\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Sawyer, an articulate fountain of ideas, is the genre's northern star -- in fact, one of the hottest SF writers anywhere.  By any reckoning Sawyer is among the most successful Canadian authors ever." --\b \i Maclean's: Canada's National Magazine\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Robert J. Sawyer is a writer of boundless confidence and bold scientific extrapolation." --\b \i The New York Times\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Sawyer's books -- always rich in science, action, and profound thinking -- never fail to surprise, delight, and cause us to transcend our ordinary thinking.  I've read Crichton, Asimov, Heinlein, Clarke, King, and Koontz -- and Sawyer outdoes them all." --\b Clifford A. Pickover\b0 , author of \b \i Time: A Traveler's Guide\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "A polished, exciting writer.  Sawyer writes with the scientific panache and grandeur of Arthur C. Clarke and the human touch of Isaac Asimov." --\b \i Quill & Quire\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Cracking open a new Robert J. Sawyer book is like getting a gift from a friend who visits all the strange and undiscovered places in the world.  You can't wait to see what he's going to amaze you with this time." --\b \i John Scalzi\b0 \i0 , author of \b Old Man's War\b0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Sawyer is Canada's answer to Michael Crichton." --\b \i The Toronto Star\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "No reader seeking well-written stories that respect, emphasize and depend on modern science should be disappointed by the works of Rob Sawyer." --\b \i The Washington Post\b0 \i0 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 }\par
\pard\plain\kerning1\ql\li0\ri0\sl-480\slmult0\tx720\tx4752{\f0\fs24 \cf1 "Sawyer is a terrific writer.  He can write about the most sophisticated science while giving readers the room to understand what's happening and follow the plot." --\b \i Winnipeg Free Press\b0 \i0 }\par
